{"cuisine/cherokee-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cherokee-foodways","n":"Cherokee foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Northeastern Oklahoma and western North Carolina","names":"Cherokee","intro":[["Cherokee people live in northeastern Oklahoma, home of the Cherokee Nation, and in western North Carolina, home of the Eastern Band of Cherokee Indians. ",["Kanuchi","dish/kanuchi"],", a soup made from pounded hickory nuts, is still served at traditional tables and stomp grounds in Oklahoma, and the nuts are gathered in fall before the first frost."],["Spring wild onion dinners bring wild onions and eggs together with ",["hominy","ingredient/hominy"],", beans, grape dumplings, kanuchi, fry bread and corn bread. In North Carolina, cooks simmer a ",["Three Sisters","ingredient/three-sisters-agriculture"]," stew of corn, beans and squash, and make an old style chestnut bread: corn cooked with wood ash, ground, mixed with chestnuts, wrapped in leaves and boiled."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Cherokee Phoenix, Hickory nuts mean kanuchi","url":"https://www.cherokeephoenix.org/health/hickory-nuts-mean-kanuchi/article_fdebe40b-9b48-5849-bf6a-1601baaecbc7.html"},{"name":"Tahlequah Daily Press, Cherokee people know how to come by tribal food","url":"https://www.tahlequahdailypress.com/news/5ws-1h-cherokee-people-know-how-to-come-by-tribal-food/article_493cf0d6-64b3-57d7-807c-e6c77af876e5.html"},{"name":"Eastern Band of Cherokee Indians, Cherokee Tribal Food Distribution Program recipes","url":"https://food.ebci-nsn.gov/?p=4673"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cherokee-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Frybread","dish/frybread"],["Kanuchi","dish/kanuchi"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Cherokee Tribal Food Distribution Program","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"food.ebci-nsn.gov","note":"Traditional recipes from the Eastern Band of Cherokee Indians, like Three Sisters stew and chestnut bread.","url":"https://food.ebci-nsn.gov/?p=4673","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8c/Frybread.jpg/500px-Frybread.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frybread.jpg","of":"Frybread"}},"cuisine/zapotec-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/zapotec-foodways","n":"Zapotec foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Central Valleys of Oaxaca, Mexico","names":"Zapotec","intro":[["In Oaxaca's Central Valleys, Zapotec women make and sell ",["Tejate","dish/tejate"],", a cool drink of ",["nixtamalized","ingredient/nixtamalization"]," corn, toasted ",["Cacao","ingredient/theobroma-cacao"],", mamey seeds and rosita de cacao, a fragrant flower. Everything is ground into a dough and slowly thinned with water until pale curds float on top, and it's served in jícaras, painted gourd bowls. San Andrés Huayápam, which has grown rosita de cacao since the 1500s, holds a tejate fair with about 150 vendors."],["Abigail Mendoza Ruiz, a Zapotec cook, has run her restaurant Tlamanalli since 1990. She cooks ",["moles","dish/mole-sauce"],", among them a pre-Hispanic mole with yerba santa, pipián of pumpkin seeds, and chicken broth with fresh corn and squash blossoms, and she gathered her recipes in a book called Dishdaa'w."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Roads & Kingdoms, A hundred ladies stirring chocolate drinks","url":"https://roadsandkingdoms.com/2016/hundred-ladies-stirring-chocolate-drinks/"},{"name":"Remezcla, Abigail Mendoza Ruiz wants to take Zapotec cuisine to every corner of the world","url":"https://remezcla.com/features/food/abigail-mendoza-ruiz-wants-to-take-zapotec-cuisine-to-every-corner-of-the-world"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"zapotec-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Mole","dish/mole-sauce"],["Tejate","dish/tejate"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Theobroma cacao","ingredient/theobroma-cacao"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Masa","ingredient/masa"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/30/ChickRedMole.JPG/500px-ChickRedMole.JPG","w":500,"h":226,"by":"AlejandroLinaresGarcia","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChickRedMole.JPG","of":"Mole"}},"ingredient/carrots":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/carrots","n":"Carrots","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["The carrot (Daucus carota subsp. sativus) is a root vegetable in the umbellifer family Apiaceae. Carrots are typically orange in colour due to their beta-carotene content, which makes them a rich source of vitamin A. Heirloom variants may be white, yellow, red, purple, or black. They are biennial plants."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-carrots"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrots"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Afghan cuisine","cuisine/afghan-cuisine",[["Kabuli pulao","dish/kabuli-