te":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/busiate","n":"Busiate","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Sicilian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Busiate or busiati are a type of long macaroni, originating in the province of Trapani and typical of the Calabria and Sicily regions of Italy. They take their name from busa, the Sicilian word for the stem of Ampelodesmos mauritanicus, a local grass, which is used in preparing them and giving them their helical shape. Busiate was officially included in the 2026 edition of the Italian dictionary Zingarelli."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-busiate"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Busiate"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Durum","ingredient/durum"]]},{"h":"More from Sicilian cuisine","links":[["Cassatella di Agira","dish/cassatella-di-agira"],["Crocchè","dish/crocche"],["Iris (pastry)","dish/iris-pastry"],["Maccu","dish/maccu"],["Panella","dish/panella"],["Pani câ meusa","dish/pani-ca-meusa"],["Parmigiana","dish/parmigiana"],["Pasta alla Norma","dish/pasta-alla-norma"],["Pasta con le sarde","dish/pasta-con-le-sarde"],["Pasta mollicata","dish/pasta-mollicata"],["Stigghiola","dish/stigghiola"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/98/Busiate.jpg/500px-Busiate.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busiate.jpg"}},"dish/ensaimada":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ensaimada","n":"Ensaïmada","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Balearic cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["The ensaïmada is a bread product from Mallorca, Balearic Islands, Spain, commonly found in southwestern Europe, Latin America and the Philippines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ensaimada"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ensa%C3%AFmada"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Balearic cuisine","cuisine/balearic-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Mother dough","ingredient/mother-dough"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a7/Ensaimada_DSCN0885.jpg/500px-Ensaimada_DSCN0885.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Lisa Stevens from Sutton Coldfield, UK","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ensaimada_DSCN0885.jpg"}},"ingredient/ground-beef":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ground-beef","n":"Ground beef","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Ground beef, hamburger meat (North American English), minced beef or beef mince (Commonwealth English; often just generically referred to as mince or mincemeat) is beef that has been finely chopped with a knife or meat grinder (North American), i.e., mincer or mincing machine (Commonwealth). It is used in many recipes including hamburgers, bolognese sauce, meatloaf, meatballs, kofta, and burritos."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ground-beef"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ground_beef"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Johnny Marzetti","dish/johnny-marzetti"]]],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Nikujaga","dish/nikujaga"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Macaroni","ingredient/macaroni"],["Mirin","ingredient/mirin"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d1/Hackfleisch-1.jpg/500px-Hackfleisch-1.jpg","w":500,"h":381,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Rainer Zenz assumed (based on copyright claims).","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hackfleisch-1.jpg"}},"dish/laziji":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/laziji","n":"Laziji","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Sichuan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Laziji is one of the most iconic dishes of Sichuan cuisine. It consists of small pieces of marinated chicken that are deep-fried and then stir-fried with massive quantities of dried Sichuan pepper, dried red chilies, garlic, and ginger. Originating from the Geleshan region of Chongqing, it is a staple of Jianghu cuisine (a type of Chongqing folk cooking) and is renowned for its intense mala (numbing and spicy) flavor profile."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-laziji"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laziji"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Sichuan cuisine","cuisine/sichuan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Sichuan pepper","ingredient/sichuan-pepper"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"China Sichuan Food","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chinasichuanfood.com","note":"Elaine's blog of Chinese home recipes with a strong Sichuan focus.","url":"https://www.chinasichuanfood.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"The Mala Market Blog","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"blog.themalamarket.com","note":"Recipes and ingredient guides from a US importer of Sichuan pantry staples.","url":"https://blog.themalamarket.com/mala-market-blog-about-page/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Sichuan cuisine","links":[["Doubanjiang","dish/doubanjiang"],["Fish with pickled mustard greens","dish/fish-with-pickled-mustard-greens"],["Kuanfen noodles","dish/kuanfen-noodles"],["Suan la fen","dish/suan-la-fen"],["Yuxiang shredded pork","dish/yuxiang-shredded-pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9e/Laziji_at_Beijing_Yibin_Hostel_%2820210625111300%29.jpg/500px-Laziji_at_Beijing_Yibin_Hostel_%2820210625111300%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"N509FZ","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laziji_at_Beijing_Yibin_Hostel_(20210625111300).jpg"}},"ingredient/konjac":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/konjac","n":"Konjac","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Amorphophallus konjac, commonly known as juruo, konnyaku or konjac, or moyu (Chinese: 魔芋; pinyin: móyù), is a species of flowering plant in the family Araceae. In English, it is also referred to as devil's tongue, voodoo lily, snake palm, or elephant yam."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-konjac"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konjac"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Oden","dish/oden"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Daikon","ingredient/daikon"],["Dashi","ingredient/dashi"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Fishcake","ingredient/fishcake"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/eb/Amorphophallus_konjac_CBM.png/500px-Amorphophallus_konjac_CBM.png","w":500,"h":614,"by":"Walter Hood Fitch","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amorphophallus_konjac_CBM.png"}},"dish/pancake":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pancake","n":"Pancake","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 5 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A pancake, also known as a hotcake, griddlecake, or flapjack, is a flat type of batter bread like cake, often thin and round, prepared from a starch-based batter that may contain eggs, milk, and butter, and then cooked on a hot surface such as a griddle or frying pan. Archaeological evidence suggests that pancakes were probably eaten in prehistoric societies."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pancake"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pancake"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Austrian cuisine","cuisine/austrian-cuisine"],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Vienna Sunday Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vienna-sunday.kitchen","note":"Viennese food blog of Austrian home cooking and pastries, with English-language recipes.","url":"https://www.vienna-sunday.kitchen/newest-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Lil Vienna","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lilvienna.com","note":"Austrian recipes from Ursula and David, two Austrians living in Boston.","url":"https://www.lilvienna.com/?p=1366","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Austrian cuisine","links":[["Blini","dish/blini"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chicken paprikash","dish/chicken-paprikash"],["Dampfnudel","dish/dampfnudel"],["Egg puffs and soup pearls","dish/egg-puffs-and-soup-pearls"],["Gulyásleves","dish/gulyasleves"],["Leberkäse","dish/leberkase"],["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"],["Potato salad","dish/potato-salad"],["Sachertorte","dish/sachertorte"],["Spätzle","dish/spatzle"],["Strudel","dish/strudel"],["Tarhonya","dish/tarhonya"],["Zopf","dish/zopf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e7/Cr%C3%AApe_opened_up.jpg/500px-Cr%C3%AApe_opened_up.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cr%C3%AApe_opened_up.jpg"}},"ingredient/alpinia-officinarum":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/alpinia-officinarum","n":"Alpinia officinarum","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Alpinia officinarum, known as lesser galangal, is a plant in the ginger family, cultivated in Southeast Asia. It originated in China, where its name ultimately derives. It can grow 1.5 to 2 m high, with long leaves and reddish-white flowers. The rhizomes, known as galangal, are valued for their sweet spicy flavor and aromatic scent. These are used throughout Asia in curries and perfumes, and were previously used widely in Europe. They are also used as a herbal remedy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-alpinia-officinarum"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpinia_officinarum"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Saksang","dish/saksang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Andaliman","ingredient/andaliman"],["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Dog meat","ingredient/dog-meat"],["Flavor enhancer","ingredient/flavor-enhancer"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Grated coconut","ingredient/grated-co